 Specular only (the rest of this subsection is the plane-earth form, which the curved model reduces to within 2 km): diffuse scatter is neglected, so null filling is underestimated; antenna motion with the waves is not modelled (the roughness term stands in). Antenna gain 2 dBi and 3 dB misc loss
 are **assumed**; the 900 MHz sensitivity, -121 dBm, is the RFD900x datasheet's value at the lowest air data rate, the best case of the radio, and is **assumed** because the lab's air rate is not known (review round 16, M-6, DECISIONS 889: each 6 dB lost to a faster rate, -111 dBm at 12 kbps and about -98 dBm at 200 kbps, is 6 dB less margin; QUESTIONS_FOR_LAB 12b). `two_ray_loss_db` gives the roughness factor the grazing angle `arctan2(ht + hr, d)`, whose sine is the `sin(psi)` above (review round 3, M-14: it used to pass `sin(psi)` to a function that sines its argument). The member's own Hs (perturbed) feeds the roughness; for an RF link between two nodes it is, at each moment, the Hs at each of the link's two ends (`stage1._link_hs`; review round 12, M-11, DECISIONS 651), and the margin is the worse of the two margins (review round 15, M-3, DECISIONS 834: the larger Hs is not the conservative one, because a rougher sea lowers the reflection coefficient r, which fills the interference nulls, by up to 20 dB at ht 15 m and hr 60 m, and lowers the peaks), so the margin does not depend on which end the mission names first (with Hs 0.2 m at the ship and 3 m at the USV the two namings differed by 0.3 dB at 3 km), and the ledger's `hs_filled` counts the filled samples of both ends. On the demo the change is at most 5.3e-4 dB in any sample of the usv-uas-rf margin (and the round 15 change 0.000588 dB in 595 of 78,784 samples). The antenna height of an aircraft is deck + flight altitude + 0.1 m. The end-point bracket is the documented approximation, not a measurement at the specular point: for a USV at 2.2 m and a UAS at 108 m over 30 km the specular point is 0.6 km from the USV, and the Hs at the two ends brackets the Hs there (review round 16, M-13, DECISIONS 900).
 
-**Low antennas** (review round 17, M-5 and M-6, DECISIONS 925, 926; the model is unchanged). A platform's RF antenna height is 0.1 m or above and the loader refuses a lower one (a height of 0 gave a finite margin of -43 dB at 5 km, neither 'no link' nor an error); below it the two-ray model has no meaning. Above the floor the reflection coefficient of -1 makes the field of a low antenna vanish with its height, where the sea's own coefficient leaves a floor, so the loss is overstated and the margin is too low, never too high: against a Fresnel two-ray with the assumed sea (eps_c 72.8 - j118.9 at 915 MHz, vertical polarisation; flat earth, the other antenna 15 m, 300 m to 12 km, measured by batch D, `tests/test_round17_d.py`), the model minus Fresnel is -3.3 to +0.2 dB (median -0.4) at 2.2 m, -2.9 to +2.2 (+1.3) at 0.5 m, +1.2 to +6.0 (+5.1) at 0.3 m, the Slocum's antenna, and +11.4 to +16.0 (+15.1) at 0.1 m. Reviewer B's parabolic-equation field agrees in direction and was not re-run. A margin to a Slocum can therefore show a GO as MARGINAL; the link card says so (limitation L23). The shipped UAS and USV antennas are above 0.5 m.
+**Low antennas** (review round 17, M-5 and M-6, DECISIONS 925, 926; restated by pair in round 18, M-3; the model is unchanged). A platform's RF antenna height is 0.1 m or above and the loader refuses a lower one (a height of 0 gave a finite margin of -43 dB at 5 km, neither 'no link' nor an error); below it the two-ray model has no meaning. Above the floor the reflection coefficient of -1 makes the field of a low antenna vanish with its height, where the sea's own coefficient leaves a floor, and the error is of the pair of heights, not of one antenna. Against a Fresnel two-ray with the assumed sea (eps_c 72.8 - j118.9 at 915 MHz, vertical polarisation, flat earth, Hs 0; a bound, since it has no surface wave) over ONE range for every row, 300 m to the pair's radio horizon or 12 km, 400 points (`tests/test_round18_b.py` recomputes every figure and every bound below), the model minus Fresnel, minimum to maximum (median), is: with a 15 m partner -3.3 to +0.2 dB (-0.4) at 2.2 m, -2.9 to +2.2 (+1.3) at 0.5 m, +1.2 to +6.0 (+5.1) at 0.3 m, the Slocum's antenna, and +11.4 to +16.0 (+15.1) at 0.1 m; with both ends low +9.0 to +9.3 (+9.3) for the Seaglider's 0.5 m with the Slocum's 0.3 m, +11.3 to +11.4 (+11.4) for two Slocums and +6.4 to +6.7 (+6.7) for two Seagliders; with a 2.2 m partner (the USV's whip) +5.5 to +6.2 (+6.2) at 0.3 m, +1.8 to +2.7 (+2.6) at 0.5 m, and -2.4 to +1.6 (-1.2) for two 2.2 m ends. Reviewer B's parabolic-equation field (round 18, 15 m partner, 300 m to 12 km; not re-run) agrees to about 1 dB for the 15 m rows and gives +3.7 dB at the median for the Slocum with a 2.2 m end. So the loss is overstated and the margin too low for an antenna below 0.5 m (a GO can show as MARGINAL or NO-GO): about 5 dB for a Slocum with the USV, 6 to 11 dB for two gliders (an RF link between two gliders is accepted by the loader). At 0.5 m and 2.2 m, with a 2.2 m or 15 m partner, the model is within 3.3 dB over that range and at worst understates the loss (by 2.9 dB at 0.5 m with a 15 m partner, 2.4 dB for two 2.2 m ends, 3.3 dB at 2.2 m with a 15 m partner), so a margin can be too high there, the USV's whip included; round 17 wrote 'too low, never too high' and 'the shipped UAS and USV antennas are not affected', and both were wrong (round 18, M-3). Nearer than 300 m none of this is stated: from 200 m the 0.5 m case with a 15 m partner is understated by up to 5.0 dB, the 0.3 m case by up to 0.6 dB, and the 2.2 m case is overstated by +31.9 dB at one null of the grid. The link card says so (limitation L23). Not run: a parabolic equation for two low ends, any distance below 200 m, and Hs above 0.
 
 **The reflection coefficient -1 and the Fresnel coefficient** (review round 12, M-12, DECISIONS 652; documents only, the model is unchanged). -1 is the limit of the Fresnel coefficient G of a vertical-polarised wave on sea water as the grazing angle goes to 0 and a smooth-sea idealisation everywhere else; the earlier text, "valid below 1 to 2 deg", was wrong. For sea water at 915 MHz with eps_c = 72.8 - j118.9 (T 29 C, S 35, sigma 6.05 S/m, which gives the eps'' = 60 lambda sigma = 118.9 quoted: **assumed**, the lab has given neither the sea temperature nor the antenna polarisation, QUESTIONS_FOR_LAB 12a) the magnitude of G is 0.98, 0.91, 0.84, 0.70, 0.49, 0.35, 0.26, 0.49 and 0.65 at 0.05, 0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 3, 5, 12 and 20 deg, and its phase is -179, -168, -154 and -86 deg at 0.05, 1, 2 and 5 deg (pseudo-Brewster angle 4.85 deg, magnitude 0.26): -1 is within 10 % of the magnitude only below about 0.3 deg (`tests/test_comms.py::test_r12_m12_*` recomputes the table). The demo's grazing angles (UAS 18 to 108 m, whip 2.2 m) are 1.2 deg at 1 km and 0.2 deg at 5 km for the 18 m UAS and 6.3 and 1.3 deg for the 108 m UAS, and 2 to 30 deg for the UAS-ship hook inside a sortie (|G| is 0.26 at 5 deg and 0.65 at 20 deg), so -1 is within 10 % of the magnitude from about 4 km out for the 18 m UAS (0.3 deg at 3.9 km, flat earth) and from about 21 km for the 108 m UAS, and not inside those distances. Error band of the margin against Fresnel, as the reviewer measured it (a script run outside the repository with this module's geometry and G(psi) in place of -1, which with -1 reproduces the function to 0.0 dB; the margin sweeps were not re-run when this was written): the -1 model is optimistic at interference peaks by up to 7.6 dB and conservative at nulls; on the mission link (UAS to USV, 0.2 km to the horizon) the Fresnel margin is 7.4 dB lower to 23.6 dB higher at Hs 0.5 m and 2.8 dB lower to 6.9 dB higher at Hs 1.5 m, on the hook (0.05 to 6 km, Hs 0.5 m) 7.5 dB lower to 34.7 dB higher, and no sample changed its sign. No verdict of the demo rests on it (usv-uas-rf is NO-GO because 88.85 % of its needed moments are beyond the radio horizon, `frac_beyond_horizon` 0.88848 in q12, and the UAS lost-link mode is 0 of 64), but a link whose budget is within about 8 dB at a peak is not decided by this function. A Fresnel function in place of -1 inside `two_ray_curved_db` would move every printed RF margin, the goldens and the parameter-file hashes, and needs a sea temperature and salinity from the lab.
 
